# 🔤 Character Escape Codes in Java

> **Escape sequences** are special character combinations used to represent characters that cannot be typed directly using the keyboard.

They are written using a **backslash (`\`)** followed by a character.

---

## 🔢 Common Escape Sequences

| Code | Description            |
| ---- | ---------------------- |
| `\n` | New Line               |
| `\t` | Tab (horizontal space) |
| `\b` | Backspace              |
| `\r` | Carriage Return        |
| `\\` | Backslash (`\`)        |
| `\'` | Single Quote (`'`)     |
| `\"` | Double Quote (`"`)     |

---

## ⚙️ Explanation

### 🔽 New Line (`\n`)

* Moves cursor to next line

```java id="c1"
System.out.println("Hello\nWorld");
```

---

### 🔲 Tab (`\t`)

* Adds horizontal spacing

```java id="c2"
System.out.println("Hello\tWorld");
```

---

### 🔙 Backspace (`\b`)

* Removes previous character

```java id="c3"
System.out.println("Helloo\b");
```

---

### 🔁 Carriage Return (`\r`)

* Moves cursor to beginning of line

```java id="c4"
System.out.println("Hello\rJava");
```

---

### 🔗 Backslash (`\\`)

* Prints a single backslash

```java id="c5"
System.out.println("C:\\Users\\Admin");
```

---

### 🔸 Quotes (`\'` and `\"`)

* Used to print quotation marks

```java id="c6"
System.out.println("She said \"Hello\"");
System.out.println('\'');
```

---

## 🔢 Special Numeric Escape Sequences

### 1. Octal Escape (`\ooo`)

* Represents character using **octal (base 8) value**

```java id="c7"
System.out.println("\101");  // Output: A
```

---

### 2. Unicode Escape (`\uXXXX`)

* Represents character using **Unicode (hexadecimal)**

```java id="c8"
System.out.println("\u2122");  // ™ (Trademark)
```

---

## ⚠️ Important Notes

* Escape sequences are mainly used in:

  * Strings (`" "`)
  * Character literals (`' '`)

* `\x` (hex escape like C/C++) ❌ **Not supported in Java**

* Unicode (`\u`) is the standard way for special characters

---

## 🚀 Quick Example

```java id="c9"
class Example6 {
    public static void main(String args[]) {

        System.out.println("Hello\nWorld");
        System.out.println("Java\tProgramming");
        System.out.println("Path: C:\\Files\\Java");

        System.out.println("Trademark: \u2122");
        System.out.println("Octal A: \101");
    }
}
```

---

# ✅ Summary

* Escape codes start with `\`
* Used for **special/non-typable characters**
* Common ones: `\n`, `\t`, `\\`, `\"`, `\'`
* Unicode (`\uXXXX`) is most powerful
* `\x` is **not valid in Java**

---
